---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title=".folder — mission" description="Why .folder/ exists: one shared directory for all dev tool configs. No more dotfolder collisions.">
    <main class="dashboard-page mission-page">
        <h1
            style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:0.4rem;"
        >
            why .folder/
        </h1>
        <p class="mission-lede">
            The problem is simple. The solution should be too.
        </p>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>the mess at your repo root</h2>
            <p>
                Every developer tool wants its own dotfolder. Your repo root
                ends up littered with <code>.sisyphus/</code>, <code
                    >.vscode/</code
                >,
                <code>.idea/</code>, <code>.astro/</code>, <code
                    >.wrangler/</code
                >, and six more you forgot to gitignore. Each one is
                invisible-by-convention, impossible to discover, and guaranteed
                to collide with the next tool that picks the same name.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>one folder to hold them all</h2>
            <p>
                <code>.folder/</code> is a single, shared directory at your repo root.
                Instead of scattering state everywhere, tools store their data in
                a named subdirectory — <code>.folder/cursor/</code>,
                <code>.folder/biome/</code>, <code>.folder/yourTool/</code>.
                Everything in one place, discoverable and organized.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>what about git?</h2>
            <p>
                Some tool state should be tracked — project rules, shared
                config, anything the team needs to stay in sync. Other state is
                ephemeral — caches, logs, runtime artifacts. <code
                    >.folder/</code
                > handles both. Gitignore the whole directory and un-ignore what matters,
                track everything and ignore specific subdirectories, or mix and match.
                The convention is about <em>where</em> things live, not
                <em>whether</em> they're committed.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>why a registry?</h2>
            <p>
                If two tools pick the same subdirectory name, their data
                collides and things break. The .folder registry is a
                lightweight, public record of which names are taken — so you can
                look up a namespace before you ship, claim it, and know it won't
                conflict with anyone else. Think of it as DNS for your repo's
                local state.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>how it works</h2>
            <ol class="mission-steps">
                <li>
                    <strong>Look up</strong> a namespace to check if it's available.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sign in</strong> with GitHub to verify your identity.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Claim</strong> your namespace with a project name, type,
                    and URL.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Ship</strong> your tool — anyone can search the registry
                    to see what lives under <code>.folder/yourName/</code>.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>who is this for?</h2>
            <p>
                Anyone building developer tools that need to store per-repo data
                — CLI tools, MCP servers, editor plugins, SDKs, and anything
                else that currently drops a dotfolder in your project root. If
                your tool writes files to the user's repo, it belongs in <code
                    >.folder/</code
                >.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mission-section">
            <h2>principles</h2>
            <ul class="mission-principles">
                <li>
                    <strong>Convention over configuration.</strong> No spec to implement,
                    no SDK to install. Just put your stuff in <code
                        >.folder/yourName/</code
                    >.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Opt-in, not mandated.</strong> The registry is a coordination
                    layer, not a gatekeeper. You don't need permission to use
                    <code>.folder/</code> — but registering prevents headaches.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Minimal by design.</strong> We store a name, a type, a
                    URL, and who claimed it. Nothing else. No analytics, no tracking,
                    no lock-in.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <div class="mission-cta">
            <a
                href="/"
                class="btn btn-primary"
                style="max-width:280px;margin:0 auto;"
            >
                look up a namespace →
            </a>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<style>
    .mission-page {
        max-width: 600px;
    }

    .mission-lede {
        font-family: var(--serif);
        font-style: italic;
        font-size: 1rem;
        color: var(--muted);
        margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
    }

    .mission-section {
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .mission-section h2 {
        font-family: var(--serif);
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
        color: var(--text);
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    }

    .mission-section p {
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        color: var(--muted);
        line-height: 1.9;
    }

    .mission-section code {
        font-family: var(--mono);
        font-size: 0.72rem;
        background: var(--bg2);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        padding: 0.08rem 0.28rem;
        border-radius: 3px;
        color: var(--text);
    }

    .mission-steps {
        list-style: none;
        counter-reset: steps;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .mission-steps li {
        counter-increment: steps;
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        color: var(--muted);
        line-height: 1.9;
        padding-left: 1.6rem;
        position: relative;
        margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
    }

    .mission-steps li::before {
        content: counter(steps);
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        font-family: var(--serif);
        font-style: italic;
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        color: var(--muted2);
        top: 0.04rem;
    }

    .mission-steps li strong {
        color: var(--text);
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .mission-principles {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .mission-principles li {
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        color: var(--muted);
        line-height: 1.9;
        padding-left: 1rem;
        position: relative;
        margin-bottom: 0.55rem;
    }

    .mission-principles li::before {
        content: "—";
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        color: var(--muted2);
    }

    .mission-principles li strong {
        color: var(--text);
        font-weight: 500;
    }

    .mission-cta {
        margin-top: 2.5rem;
        padding-top: 2rem;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
        display: flex;
        justify-content: center;
    }
</style>
